You can see the complete code in cpp_state_spaces.cpp.::: { .callout-note }The compiled module resolves phasic’s ptd_* C symbols (and the thread-local ptd_err) from the already-loaded phasic_pybind extension. CPython imports extensions with RTLD_LOCAL, so on Linux those symbols are not in the global dynamic-linker scope by default and the import would fail with undefined symbol: ptd_err. The cell below first re-opens phasic_pybind with RTLD_GLOBAL to promote its symbols; do the same in your own code before importing a cppimport module built this way.:::

# The cpp_state_spaces module is linked against phasiccpp.cpp and references# phasic's C API — including the thread-local `ptd_err`, which the dynamic# linker must resolve eagerly at load. CPython imports extensions with# RTLD_LOCAL, so those symbols are NOT in the global scope by default and the# import fails with "undefined symbol: ptd_err". Re-open phasic_pybind with# RTLD_GLOBAL first to promote its symbols into the global namespace.import os, glob, ctypes, phasicif hasattr(os, "RTLD_GLOBAL"):    _pb = glob.glob(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(phasic.__file__), "phasic_pybind*.so"))    if _pb:        ctypes.CDLL(_pb[0], mode=os.RTLD_NOW | os.RTLD_GLOBAL)import cppimport.import_hookimport cpp_state_spaces # import your extensiongraph = Graph(cpp_state_spaces.coalescent(4)) # wrap the returned pybind11 object in Graphgraph.plot()


Callback-based construction in C++The C++ API mirrors the Python callback interface directly with Graph::from_callback, so you get the same convenience as Graph(callback, ipv=...) without writing the breadth-first exploration loop by hand. The transition callback returns a std::vector&lt;phasic::Transition&gt; — each Transition is a target state, a weight, and optional coefficients (non-empty for parameterised edges) — and Graph::from_callback explores the state space for you:cppphasic::Graph coalescent(int nr_samples) {  std::vector&lt;int&gt; initial_state(nr_samples, 0);  initial_state[0] = nr_samples;                                   // [nr_samples, 0, 0, ...]  std::vector&lt;std::pair&lt;std::vector&lt;int&gt;, double&gt;&gt; ipv = {{initial_state, 1.0}};  auto callback =      [](const std::vector&lt;int&gt; &state) -&gt; std::vector&lt;phasic::Transition&gt; {    std::vector&lt;phasic::Transition&gt; transitions;    int size = (int) state.size();    for (int i = 0; i &lt; size; ++i)      for (int j = i; j &lt; size; ++j) {        int same = (i == j) ? 1 : 0;        if (same && state[i] &lt; 2) continue;        if (!same && (state[i] &lt; 1 || state[j] &lt; 1)) continue;        std::vector&lt;int&gt; next = state;                             // state.copy()        next[i] -= 1; next[j] -= 1; next[i + j + 1] += 1;        double rate = (double) state[i] * (state[j] - same) / (1 + same);        transitions.emplace_back(next, rate);                      // constant edge      }    return transitions;  };  return phasic::Graph::from_callback(nr_samples, ipv, callback);}The complete module (with the same // cppimport header/footer as above) is in cpp_callback_construction.cpp. For a standalone program compiled directly with clang++ (no Python), see ../examples/cpp/coalescent_from_callback.cpp.

